  {
   "tone": "light",
   "html": "<style>.tl{position:absolute;inset:0;background:linear-gradient(180deg,var(--bg) 0%,color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 5%,var(--bg)) 100%)}.tl .rows{position:absolute;inset:0;background:repeating-linear-gradient(180deg,color-mix(in srgb,var(--accent) 7%,transparent) 0 2px,transparent 2px 72px)}.tl .ruler{position:absolute;inset:0;background:repeating-linear-gradient(90deg,color-mix(in srgb,var(--text) 5%,transparent) 0 1px,transparent 1px 128px)}</style><div class='tl'><div class='rows'></div><div class='ruler'></div></div>",
   "_why": "DELIBERATELY STILL for the whole reel. This is a motion reference: the viewer compares one entrance against the last, so the only thing moving in frame must be the effect under examination. A travelling playhead lived here and was cut - it carried no information and it pulled the eye sideways during every beat. The grid stays because a still workspace ground gives the white frame texture without competing.",
   "from": 0,
   "to": "cut@9"
  },
  {
   "preset": "ink",
   "_why": "the one turn in the film, and it lands on the payoff: the workshop light goes out and the answer is left on a dark field.",
   "from": "cut@9",
   "to": 32.5
  }
 ],
 "audio": {
  "auto": true,
  "sfxGain": 0.55,
  "_why": "no bed and no voice: the reel is read frame by frame, and a track would set a pace the viewer is meant to ignore while comparing nine entrances. The engine's own cut cues punctuate the ten joints, so the metric rhythm is audible without a loop."
 },
